# Example workflow: triage issues/PRs/comments/discussions on YOUR OWN repo
# (e.g. your fork, once you treat it as its own project with real
# collaborators). This is "owner mode": the repo being watched is the same
# repo that owns the credentials running this job, so auto-posting triage
# comments and PR reviews is reasonable — see SKILL.md section 6a.
#
# What this still does NOT do automatically: merge PRs, close issues/PRs, or
# change repo settings. Those stay manual, on purpose — bot-assisted triage is
# normal; bot-assisted merging is how you end up shipping something nobody
# reviewed.
#
# One-time setup before this does anything useful:
#   1. In your fork's Settings, enable "Issues" and "Discussions" (forks have
#      both disabled by default), so your friends have somewhere to post.
#   2. Add your friends as the repo's audience: either leave the repo public
#      (anyone can open issues/PRs/discussions on a public repo) or add them
#      as collaborators if you want write access for them too.
#   3. Add an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` repository secret (Settings > Secrets and
#      variables > Actions). This uses Anthropic's official, documented
#      `claude-code-action`, so unlike the Devin CLI version, CI auth here is
#      confirmed and supported, not a guess.
#   4. Commit `.claude/skills/apache-review/SKILL.md` into this repo (same
#      content as the global one) so the action can find and use it.
# No environment-approval gate: comments post immediately when this runs,
# by design (see "Keep auto-posting comments" decision). An environment
# protection rule was considered, but it would only let you approve/reject a
# whole BLIND run before it executes — it can't show you draft comment text
# first, since that text doesn't exist until the job runs. Given comments are
# meant to auto-post, that gate would only add delay, not real review.
#
# The actual daily review loop is: PRs this opens sit open, with their diff
# visible, until you merge them — that review step already exists natively
# and needs nothing extra. Check your fork's Issues/PRs/Discussions tabs (and
# the Actions tab, to see what ran) whenever you log in; there's no separate
# dashboard beyond GitHub's own.
#
# Safety property: `permissions` below is scoped to THIS repo only, and never
# includes repo administration or merge-bypass scopes. The job can comment,
# label, and open PRs/branches here — it cannot change who owns or administers
# the repo, and nothing here merges anything.
#
# SECURITY NOTE on `pull_request_target`: this trigger runs with write-scoped
# credentials even for PRs from a friend's own fork, which is why it's used
# here instead of `pull_request`. That combination is only safe because the
# checkout step below never checks out or executes the PR's own head commit —
# it only reads metadata via the API/`gh`. If this workflow is ever changed to
# build or run the PR's code (e.g. to run tests), that step must use a
# separate job with the `pull_request` trigger and no write token, not this
# one — otherwise a malicious PR could steal this job's credentials.

name: Fork triage

on:
  issues:
    types: [opened]
  issue_comment:
    types: [created]
  pull_request_target:
    types: [opened, synchronize]
  pull_request_review_comment:
    types: [created]
  discussion:
    types: [created]
  discussion_comment:
    types: [created]
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 13 * * *"   # daily digest/catch-all, in case an event was missed
  workflow_dispatch: {}

permissions:
  contents: write          # to create branches/commits for a proposed fix
  issues: write            # to comment/label on issues
  pull-requests: write     # to comment/review/open PRs
  discussions: write       # to reply in discussions
  id-token: write          # required by claude-code-action
  actions: read            # lets Claude read CI results on PRs
  # No admin/merge-bypass permissions, and nothing here ever calls the merge
  # or branch-protection APIs.

jobs:
  triage:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 1

      - name: Figure out what triggered this run
        id: target
        run: |
          case "${{ github.event_name }}" in
            issues) echo "url=${{ github.event.issue.html_url }}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;
            issue_comment) echo "url=${{ github.event.comment.html_url }}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;
            pull_request_target) echo "url=${{ github.event.pull_request.html_url }}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;
            pull_request_review_comment) echo "url=${{ github.event.comment.html_url }}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;
            discussion) echo "url=${{ github.event.discussion.html_url }}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;
            discussion_comment) echo "url=${{ github.event.comment.html_url }}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;
            *) echo "url=" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT" ;;  # schedule / workflow_dispatch: do a full sweep instead
          esac

      - name: Run apache-review skill (owner/triage mode)
        id: claude
        uses: anthropics/claude-code-action@v1
        with:
          anthropic_api_key: ${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
          # `prompt` makes this run automatically on the event, with no "@claude"
          # mention required — needed since friends won't know to ask for it.
          prompt: |
            Use the apache-review skill, owner/triage mode (this repo is mine).
            ${{ steps.target.outputs.url != '' &&
                format('Target: {0}. Verify it, then post a triage comment or PR review directly.', steps.target.outputs.url) ||
                format('No single target (scheduled sweep): check open issues/PRs/discussions in {0} updated since the last run, post triage comments/reviews on anything not already handled.', github.repository) }}
            Do not merge, close, or force-push anything.
